WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.856
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.750 --> 00:00:09.460
SPEAKER: The brain is a magical organ.

00:00:09.460 --> 00:00:14.600
For us to hear sound, it does
things complex and incredible,

00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:15.457
but not unknowable.

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:21.510
The brain takes sound waves
which journey through the air

00:00:21.510 --> 00:00:25.747
and through our skulls and transforms
them into sound in our minds.

00:00:28.370 --> 00:00:33.700
This sonic odyssey is beautiful,
filled with weird landscapes,

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:37.730
unexpected encounters,
catastrophe, and joyful

00:00:37.730 --> 00:00:41.277
destinations, all like a
strange aural fairy tale.

00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:48.370
Hearing evolved because it
helped animals to survive.

00:00:48.370 --> 00:00:52.020
Hearing allowed predators
to find prey and eat them,

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:55.435
and hearing allowed prey to
spy on predators and to escape.

00:00:58.930 --> 00:01:02.440
The floppy piece of skin
attached to the sides of our head

00:01:02.440 --> 00:01:05.440
is our outer ear, or pinna.

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:08.668
It collects sounds from a
wide area of our evironment,

00:01:08.668 --> 00:01:12.155
like a reflector disk
of a satellite dish.

00:01:12.155 --> 00:01:16.270
In humans, the pinna is in a fixed
position, but in many animals,

00:01:16.270 --> 00:01:21.050
the pinna are highly flexible and can
be oriented toward a source of sound.

00:01:24.482 --> 00:01:26.897
Curiously, our auditory
system has evolved

00:01:26.897 --> 00:01:29.312
beyond it's strict utility of survival.

00:01:32.693 --> 00:01:35.365
For example, music,
through its perception

00:01:35.365 --> 00:01:41.350
of sound by our auditory system,
evokes sensations and emotions.

00:01:45.850 --> 00:01:48.409
Sound is produced when
something vibrates,

00:01:48.409 --> 00:01:50.510
causing variations in air pressure.

00:01:55.490 --> 00:02:00.420
Particles of air collide with other
particles, and the wave of movement

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:05.180
bouncing from particle to particle
passes outward through the air.

00:02:05.180 --> 00:02:06.851
This is called a sound wave.

00:02:10.699 --> 00:02:13.960
And now our brave sound waves
are approaching the outer ear

00:02:13.960 --> 00:02:17.460
and are truly starting their journey.

00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:21.220
Once they have entered
the ear through the pinna,

00:02:21.220 --> 00:02:28.400
they pass down the air-filled auditory
canal before hitting the eardrum.

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:33.550
As the sound waves strike the
eardrum, the vibrations in the air

00:02:33.550 --> 00:02:38.180
are transferred through that membrane
into a series of little bones

00:02:38.180 --> 00:02:40.080
called the ossicles.

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:45.770
There are three bones in this group,
the malleus, incus, and stapes,

00:02:45.770 --> 00:02:50.390
also known as the hammer,
anvil, and stirrup.

00:02:50.390 --> 00:02:54.020
They function to transmit sounds
from the air-filled outer ear

00:02:54.020 --> 00:02:57.820
into the fluid-filled inner
ear by allowing the sound

00:02:57.820 --> 00:03:01.300
to enter them through the
eardrum, vibrate through them,

00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:05.900
and then exit into the inner ear via a
second membrane called the oval window.

00:03:10.460 --> 00:03:14.550
The inner ear contains the
sensory organs for both hearing

00:03:14.550 --> 00:03:17.480
as well as for our sense of balance.

00:03:17.480 --> 00:03:20.720
The cochlea, an important
organ for the auditory process,

00:03:20.720 --> 00:03:26.240
is snail-shaped and if rolled up,
is only about the size of a pea.

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:29.390
Inside the cochlea is
the organ of Corti,

00:03:29.390 --> 00:03:34.440
which sits on the basilar membrane
and below the tectorial membrane.

00:03:34.440 --> 00:03:39.150
And inside the organ of Corti are
thousands of auditory hair cells.

00:03:39.150 --> 00:03:45.150
These hair cells are, in fact,
receptor neurons called stereocilia.

00:03:45.150 --> 00:03:48.350
These hair cells convert the
motion energy of sound waves

00:03:48.350 --> 00:03:53.750
into electrical signals that are
communicated to the auditory nerve.

00:03:53.750 --> 00:03:58.080
From there, the signal goes into
structures deeper in the brain

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:02.620
until at last, it reaches the
auditory cortex, where we consciously

00:04:02.620 --> 00:04:05.960
experience sound.

00:04:05.960 --> 00:04:09.950
To back up, when our sound waves
enter the fluid-filled inner ear,

00:04:09.950 --> 00:04:14.520
they are thrust into a
storm, a storm of vibrations

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:16.927
that they are, in fact, creating.

00:04:16.927 --> 00:04:21.529
The basilar membrane below them is
violently shaking and tossing them.

00:04:21.529 --> 00:04:24.950
The tectorial membrane above
them is moving back and forth

00:04:24.950 --> 00:04:26.120
and pushing and threatening.

00:04:30.550 --> 00:04:33.900
And in the midst of this maelstrom,
as they are broken apart,

00:04:33.900 --> 00:04:38.400
they spiral through the inner
ear and the sound waves finally

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:40.980
begin to weaken.

00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:43.920
The inner ear absorbs all
their kinetic strength,

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:48.244
and the sound waves begin to
realize that this will be their end.

00:04:48.244 --> 00:04:54.280
Now, finally, in the chaos of
the inner ear, they disappear.

00:05:00.230 --> 00:05:06.150
The sound waves have ceased to be, but
though the vibrations are now no more,

00:05:06.150 --> 00:05:08.730
they are not completely gone.

00:05:08.730 --> 00:05:13.920
They have metamorphosed into
something new, into neural activity.

00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:16.670
And they come to life again
in the cortex of the brain.

00:05:29.870 --> 00:05:35.660
In the consciousness of our
minds, their journey is over.

00:05:35.660 --> 00:05:39.030
The brain has taken what was
outside and made it inside.

00:05:39.030 --> 00:05:42.380
[MUSIC PLAYING]

